| #        | Interview Question                                            | Short Interview Answer                                                                                                                                | 🧠 Quick Trick                                                |
| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1**    | **What is HTML?**                                             | HTML stands for **HyperText Markup Language**. It is used to **create and structure web pages**.                                                      | **HTML = Structure**                                          |
| **2**    | **HTML vs HTML5?**                                            | HTML5 is the **latest version of HTML**. It introduced semantic tags, audio, video, canvas, localStorage, etc.                                        | **HTML5 = HTML + New Features**                               |
| **3**    | **Tag, Element, Attribute?**                                  | **Tag** is markup like `<p>`. **Element** includes opening tag + content + closing tag. **Attribute** provides extra information, like `id="name"`.   | **Tag = Markup, Element = Complete, Attribute = Information** |
| **4**    | **What is `<!DOCTYPE html>`?**                                | It tells the browser that the document uses **HTML5** and helps the browser render the page correctly.                                                | **DOCTYPE = HTML5 declaration**                               |
| **5**    | **Basic HTML structure?**                                     | An HTML page mainly contains `<!DOCTYPE html>`, `<html>`, `<head>`, and `<body>`.                                                                     | **Head = Information, Body = Content**                        |
| **6**    | **`<div>` vs `<span>`?**                                      | `<div>` is a **block-level** container, while `<span>` is an **inline** container.                                                                    | **DIV = Block, SPAN = Inline**                                |
| **7**    | **`id` vs `class`?**                                          | `id` uniquely identifies an element. `class` can be used for **multiple elements**.                                                                   | **ID = One, Class = Many**                                    |
| **8**    | **Block vs Inline elements?**                                 | Block elements start on a **new line** and usually take full available width. Inline elements stay **in the same line** and take only required space. | **Block = New line, Inline = Same line**                      |
| **9**    | **What are semantic elements?**                               | Semantic elements clearly describe their **meaning and purpose**. Examples: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, `<footer>`.                | **Semantic = Meaningful**                                     |
| **10**   | **Why use `alt` in `<img>`?**                                 | `alt` provides **alternative text** if the image cannot load and improves **accessibility**.                                                          | **ALT = Alternative text**                                    |
| **11**   | **`src` vs `href`?**                                          | `src` specifies the **source of a resource**, such as an image or script. `href` specifies the **destination/link** of a resource.                    | **src = Source, href = Link**                                 |
| **12**   | **What is a hyperlink? New tab?**                             | A hyperlink connects one page/resource to another using `<a>`. To open it in a new tab, use `target="_blank"`.                                        | **`<a>` = Link**                                              |
| **13**   | **What are HTML forms?**                                      | Forms are used to **collect user input**, such as name, email, password, etc.                                                                         | **Form = Collect Data**                                       |
| **14**   | **Types of `<input>`?**                                       | Common types are `text`, `password`, `email`, `number`, `radio`, `checkbox`, `date`, `file`, and `submit`.                                            | **Input = User Data**                                         |
| **15**   | **GET vs POST?**                                              | **GET** sends data through the URL and is mainly used to retrieve data. **POST** sends data in the request body and is commonly used to submit data.  | **GET = Get, POST = Send**                                    |
| **16**   | **`disabled` vs `readonly` vs `required`?**                   | `disabled` → cannot use/edit and is generally not submitted. `readonly` → cannot edit but can be submitted. `required` → user must provide a value.   | **Disabled = No, Readonly = See, Required = Must**            |
| **17**   | **Use of `<label>`?**                                         | `<label>` gives a description/name to a form control and improves **accessibility and usability**.                                                    | **Label = Input Name**                                        |
| **18**   | **`<ul>` vs `<ol>` vs `<dl>`?**                               | `<ul>` = unordered list, `<ol>` = ordered list, `<dl>` = description list.                                                                            | **UL = Unordered, OL = Ordered, DL = Description**            |
| **19**   | **How to create a table?**                                    | Use `<table>` with rows `<tr>`, headings `<th>`, and data cells `<td>`.                                                                               | **Table → TR → TH/TD**                                        |
| **20**   | **`<tr>`, `<th>`, `<td>`?**                                   | `<tr>` creates a **table row**, `<th>` creates a **header cell**, and `<td>` creates a **data cell**.                                                 | **TR = Row, TH = Header, TD = Data**                          |
| **21**   | **`rowspan` vs `colspan`?**                                   | `rowspan` makes a cell span **multiple rows**. `colspan` makes a cell span **multiple columns**.                                                      | **ROW = Down, COL = Across**                                  |
| **22**   | **New features of HTML5?**                                    | HTML5 introduced **semantic elements, audio, video, canvas, localStorage, sessionStorage, and new form input types**.                                 | **HTML5 = Semantic + Media + Storage**                        |
| **23**   | **`header`, `nav`, `section`, `article`, `aside`, `footer`?** | These are semantic elements used to give **meaningful structure** to different parts of a webpage.                                                    | **Page Structure Tags**                                       |
| **24**   | **localStorage vs sessionStorage?**                           | `localStorage` stores data **until it is manually cleared**. `sessionStorage` stores data for the **current browser tab/session**.                    | **Local = Long, Session = Short**                             |
| **25**   | **What is DOM?**                                              | DOM stands for **Document Object Model**. It represents an HTML page as a **tree of objects**, which JavaScript can access and modify.                | **DOM = HTML as a Tree**                                      |
